\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Normal form of a saddle-node}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{Consider a scalar equation ##{\dot x=f(x,\alpha)} with an
\vocabk{equilibrium}{fnip-0014} at the origin when #{\alpha=0}.
Suppose the derivative in the state vanishes there, the second
derivative in the state is nonzero, and the derivative in the
parameter is nonzero. Invertible changes of the coordinate and of
the parameter bring the equation to
##{\dot\eta=\beta\pm\eta^{2}+O(\eta^{3}).}
The \vocabk{topological normal form}{fnip-0082} drops the cubic
remainder:
##{\dot\eta=\beta\pm\eta^{2}.}
\citet{Theorems 3.1 and 3.2, p. 84}{kuznetsov2004elements} are
these two statements. On that page the second-derivative condition
is the nondegeneracy condition, and the condition on the parameter
is the transversality condition.}

\p{In #{\mathbb{R}^{n}}, let the derivative at an equilibrium have
a simple eigenvalue #{0}, with the remaining eigenvalues off the
imaginary axis. If the left eigenvector pairs nontrivially with
the parameter derivative, and pairs nontrivially with the
quadratic term on the right eigenvector, then a smooth curve of
equilibria lies in the product of the state space and the
parameter, passes through the critical point, and is tangent to
the state space at the critical parameter. Equilibria near that
point are absent on one side of the critical parameter, and two
\vocabk{hyperbolic}{fnip-0034} equilibria exist near it on the
other.
\citet{Theorem 3.4.1, p. 148}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear} is this
statement. The paragraph introducing that theorem says that it
states the necessary conditions. The paragraph after the theorem
calls the two inequalities the transversality conditions. The
displayed theorem is the implication from its three hypotheses. Along the
\vocabk{center manifold}{fnip-0059}, the quadratic coefficient is
##{b=\tfrac{1}{2}\langle p,B(q,q)\rangle,}
where #{q} and #{p} are the right and left eigenvectors for the
eigenvalue #{0}, normalized so that #{\langle p,q\rangle=1}, and
#{B} is the quadratic term of the vector field.
\citet{(5.23), p. 175}{kuznetsov2004elements} is this coefficient.}
